The current API confirms the storage model: `credentials.json` is primary, `credentials.captured.json` is the recoverable candidate, candidate-first load is intentional, and promotion compares serialized snapshots under the storage lock before replacing primary. I’m checking whether I can implement the required cross-logon mutex with a current-user-only security descriptor using documented bindings; a session-local lock would not meet the approved concurrency guarantee.
web search: 
web search: site:docs.rs/windows-sys/latest/windows_sys/Win32/Security/Credentials/fn.CredWriteW.html ...
codex
The WinCred documentation confirms `CredReadW` binds reads to the current token’s credential set and requires `CredFree`; a `Global\` mutex is needed for cross-session coordination and named synchronization objects accept an explicit security descriptor. The required lock would therefore need a stable current-user SID lookup plus a verified ACL on a global kernel object. I haven’t established that security descriptor path from the project’s existing dependencies yet, so I’m treating this as the explicit cross-session locking blocker rather than introducing a `Local\` mutex that would silently weaken concurrency. I’ll save the concrete stopping point and commands/evidence now.
